FINAL I’ll inspect the task prompt and translator first, then build the smallest semantics that faithfully covers the translated program, validate concrete executions, and close all specifications with `kprove`. I’ll keep the command trail reproducible in `prove.sh`.
FINAL The prompt is the decimal-string rounding task. I’m choosing a very small implementation whose behavior is easy to expose in K: parse the string once, add or subtract one half according to sign, then truncate. The verification will cover the tie rule symbolically and run the supplied examples concretely.
FINAL A useful correction emerged before writing the semantics: binary `float` would make the implementation wrong for sufficiently precise decimal strings. I’m switching the Python implementation to `Decimal` while retaining the explicit add/subtract-half algorithm, so the proof can model decimal values exactly rather than silently inheriting IEEE-754 approximation.
FINAL The semantics now compiles. It interprets the translated AST (module loading, environments, evaluation order, calls, arithmetic, comparison, branching, and return) and includes an exact decimal/scientific-notation parser; I’m running boundary-heavy concrete cases before introducing symbolic claims.
